1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
We eat, and to get it, we bake, boil, and blast.

2
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:12,340
It's essential to life, but too much of it could kill you.

3
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,440
It's made from two volatile elements.

4
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:21,200
But it's a miracle mineral, ideal for everything from clearing roads.

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:23,860
If we don't do the job right, someone gets hurt.

6
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:26,480
To cleaning your pool.

7
00:00:26,481 --> 00:00:29,480
It's even perfect for burning rubber.

8
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:34,380
Once you go 200 out here, then you want to go a little faster.

9
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:37,320
Now, salt on Modern Marbles.

10
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:52,180
This unassuming mineral essential to our survival

11
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:56,480
is a mover and shaker in a class by a man.

12
00:00:56,481 --> 00:00:57,181
itself.

13
00:00:57,182 --> 00:01:01,880
It's salt, and throughout history, those controlling it have been the masters

14
00:01:01,881 --> 00:01:03,446
of their domain.

15
00:01:03,447 --> 00:01:10,200
Two of today's superpowers, China and the United States, rank one and

16
00:01:10,201 --> 00:01:16,660
two in salt production, and each nation's salt heritage runs deep. China's emperors

17
00:01:16,661 --> 00:01:22,475
financed construction of the Great Wall with profits from their lucrative salt trade.

18
00:01:22,476 --> 00:01:23,176
And

19
00:01:23,177 --> 00:01:26,920
Salt was the principal cargo on the Erie Canal as a young America pushed west.

20
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:31,240
Part of salt's value lies in its versatility.

21
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:37,920
It has 14,000 known uses, from softening water to killing poison ivy.

22
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:40,560
We all know it adds flavor to our food.

23
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:47,140
But did you know that it's a vital ingredient in glass, rubber, paint, and bug spray?

24
00:01:47,141 --> 00:01:52,920
Salt is the name that chemists give to a metal and nonmetal compound.

25
00:01:53,920 --> 00:02:00,960
The most commonly known salt is sodium chloride, or NACL, which features sodium metal and chlorine gas.

26
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:07,180
Incredibly, the two elements that comprise the stable, ingestible salt we all know and love

27
00:02:07,181 --> 00:02:11,540
are two very reactive and sometimes dangerous substances.

28
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:17,080
What we're going to do today is a very simple but exciting demonstration of adding sodium to water.

29
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:21,640
And what I've got right here are some bars of sodium metal,

30
00:02:21,641 --> 00:02:22,880
which we can cut.

31
00:02:22,881 --> 00:02:26,640
It's quite soft, actually, in its native metallic form.

32
00:02:26,641 --> 00:02:29,180
So I can cut it very easily with a knife.

33
00:02:29,181 --> 00:02:33,180
So we'll put this piece of metallic sodium into the water

34
00:02:33,181 --> 00:02:35,380
and watch what happens.

35
00:02:35,381 --> 00:02:38,540
The generation of hydrogen gas, as well as the amount of heat

36
00:02:38,541 --> 00:02:40,480
and energy being given off by the sodium,

37
00:02:40,481 --> 00:02:42,840
results in a pretty decent ignition of the hydrogen.

38
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,480
The experiment creates quite a light show,

39
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:53,740
but it's nothing compared to what happens when you add too much sodium.

40
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,960
This is a pretty big piece, so we'll see what happens.

41
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,540
It's pretty impressive.

42
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:15,100
The other element in salt, chlorine, may not be as explosive,

43
00:03:15,101 --> 00:03:17,300
but it can be equally dangerous.

44
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:21,340
In its natural state, chlorine is a gas and poisonous.

45
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:26,060
In World War I, both sides used it as a weapon,

46
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:29,800
contributing to 91,000 gas-related deaths.

47
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:34,640
Although chlorine and sodium can be deadly independent of one another,

48
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:40,500
together they form the benign substance that is ultimately a driving force of life.

49
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:43,000
We all need salt to survive.

50
00:03:43,001 --> 00:03:48,500
the adult human body contains over half a pound of it enough to fill three large

51
00:03:48,501 --> 00:03:55,900
shakers our bodies are basically big electrical machines and the salt is the

52
00:03:55,901 --> 00:04:00,280
electrolyte that keeps everything in balance keeps the electric charges going

53
00:04:00,281 --> 00:04:05,020
in and out of the cells it aids in the functioning of our nerves the brain and

54
00:04:05,021 --> 00:04:10,540
almost every other system in the body because salt is crucial to our very

55
00:04:10,541 --> 00:04:11,700
existence.

56
00:04:11,701 --> 00:04:14,480
Nature hardwired us to crave it.

57
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:17,880
Salt is one of the basic tastes, and so humans

58
00:04:17,881 --> 00:04:21,040
have a preference for salt.

59
00:04:21,041 --> 00:04:24,580
It is an innate desire to consume salt.

60
00:04:24,581 --> 00:04:27,260
It also makes foods taste better.

61
00:04:27,261 --> 00:04:29,020
It's not just a salty taste.

62
00:04:29,021 --> 00:04:31,020
It's that it masks bitter taste.

63
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:36,180
It enhances sweet taste, which makes the food more palatable.

64
00:04:36,181 --> 00:04:38,400
Since we need salt so much, it's a good thing

65
00:04:38,401 --> 00:04:44,560
we can find in almost anywhere on earth, including vast deposits in the United States.

66
00:04:44,561 --> 00:04:50,060
Hundreds of millions of years ago, the developing North American landmass was covered by prehistoric

67
00:04:50,061 --> 00:04:50,761
seas.

68
00:04:50,762 --> 00:04:56,680
As the seas evaporated, they left their salt behind, which was later buried beneath

69
00:04:56,681 --> 00:04:58,027
layers of sediment.

70
00:04:58,028 --> 00:05:03,060
One huge deposit stretches from Michigan through Ohio into Pennsylvania

71
00:05:03,061 --> 00:05:04,095
and New York.

72
00:05:04,096 --> 00:05:10,700
And it's what miners in Cleveland, Ohio, have been busily extracting at the Cargill

73
00:05:10,701 --> 00:05:12,703
Salt Mine since 1962.

74
00:05:12,704 --> 00:05:19,000
The deposit they tap, 1,800 feet directly below Lake Erie, is just

75
00:05:19,001 --> 00:05:23,059
a fraction of the mother load of salt spanning four states.

76
00:05:23,060 --> 00:05:25,260
They must drive three and a half

77
00:05:25,261 --> 00:05:35,880
miles in darkness to the excavation site.

78
00:05:35,881 --> 00:05:40,660
The salt they mine here is part of the roughly 46 million tons of salt produced annually

79
00:05:40,661 --> 00:05:47,100
in the U.S. One-fifth of the world's supply.

80
00:05:47,101 --> 00:05:53,360
Down here workers prep the blast site, where it remains a constant 70 degrees Fahrenheit.

81
00:05:53,361 --> 00:06:00,560
They remove all the loose salt and drive six-foot bolts into the roof of the chamber to reinforce it.

82
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:08,780
Then they drill holes into the wall and insert explosives.

83
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:11,960
Putting them in place takes about one hour.

84
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:17,500
Once that's complete and the area's secure, the blast is set off.

85
00:06:17,501 --> 00:06:27,280
The blast usually dislodges between 1,100 and 1,200 tons of salt, which then travels by

86
00:06:27,281 --> 00:06:33,200
conveyor to a series of crushers and screeners that reduces the chunks to less than half an

87
00:06:33,201 --> 00:06:33,960
inch in size.

88
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:39,740
When it leaves the mill, it's essentially a finished product.

89
00:06:39,741 --> 00:06:46,800
It's taken to the shaft bottom where it's loaded into our production skips, hoisted

90
00:06:46,801 --> 00:06:50,700
it to the surface at a rate of 20 tons per skip.

91
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:56,240
With three shifts working daily, the mine produces

92
00:06:56,241 --> 00:07:03,240
in excess of 13,000 tons a day and over 3 million tons each year.

93
00:07:03,241 --> 00:07:06,220
But the miners here aren't even close to exhausting their little corner

94
00:07:06,221 --> 00:07:07,760
of the underground salty stash.

95
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:14,300
We have a 9,000 acre track under lease currently.

96
00:07:14,301 --> 00:07:18,980
And essentially, with the multiple levels, it seems that we can mine.

97
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:21,700
We have 150 years of reserves remaining.

98
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:26,820
Even some of history's earliest salt mines, open and accessible to this day,

99
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:29,140
have a wealth of salt still available.

100
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:34,260
One of the oldest in Wieliczka, Poland, dates back to the 13th century.

101
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,680
Its caverns extend as deep as 1,100 feet.

102
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:42,700
At the 300-foot level, the medieval miners left an astonishing signature.

103
00:07:42,701 --> 00:07:44,920
from the vast deposit of salt.

104
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:47,420
They sculpted this chapel,

105
00:07:48,220 --> 00:07:51,160
and everything here from the chandeliers to the altar

106
00:07:51,161 --> 00:07:53,840
is made of pure salt.

107
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:01,340
In Silver Springs, New York, an hour south of Buffalo,

108
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:04,480
Morton salt uses a different mining method.

109
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:08,780
It doesn't require tons of explosives or an army of workers,

110
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:10,500
but a flood of water.

111
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:12,900
It's known as solution mining.

112
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:20,740
A pipe extends more than 2,000 feet into a subterranean salt deposit.

113
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:26,740
The pipe carries fresh water into the deposit, creating a pool of salt-intense brine.

114
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:31,540
Then, pumps force the brine up a second pipe to the surface.

115
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:36,260
Next, the brine is treated chemically to remove impurities.

116
00:08:36,261 --> 00:08:40,760
The challenge now is to separate the salt from the water.

117
00:08:40,761 --> 00:08:43,760
They do that by boiling it.

118
00:08:43,761 --> 00:08:48,260
What you're looking at right here behind me is a set of four pans,

119
00:08:48,261 --> 00:08:53,260
four evaporated pans where we bring the raw brine in or the treated brine,

120
00:08:53,261 --> 00:08:56,260
and it boils through the pan.

121
00:08:56,261 --> 00:09:00,260
The vacuum pans operate like a pressure cooker, only in reverse.

122
00:09:00,261 --> 00:09:05,260
The lower atmospheric pressure in the vacuum allows water to boil at a lower temperature.

123
00:09:05,261 --> 00:09:12,760
Water normally boils at 212 degrees Fahrenheit, but these pans can do the trick as low as

124
00:09:12,761 --> 00:09:16,020
100 degrees Fahrenheit.

125
00:09:16,021 --> 00:09:22,560
Steam from a boiler heats the first pan, boiling the brine at 225 degrees.

126
00:09:22,561 --> 00:09:29,360
That steam then travels to the second pan, where the brine boils at only 180 degrees.

127
00:09:29,361 --> 00:09:34,360
By the fourth and final pan, the brine boils at only 105 degrees.

128
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:38,360
We're now in the lower pan room.

129
00:09:38,361 --> 00:09:44,360
This is where the salt water that comes off the bottom of the evaporation pan is just called a slurry tank.

130
00:09:44,361 --> 00:09:49,360
And as you can see by looking at the brine coming out, it's a very milky color.

131
00:09:49,361 --> 00:09:52,360
And the reason it's milky is there's a lot of salt in that water.

132
00:09:52,361 --> 00:09:59,360
This is the slurry off the number four pan, which is the last of the four pans.

133
00:09:59,361 --> 00:10:04,120
The temperature has decreased so much by the time it got to this fourth pan that I can

134
00:10:04,121 --> 00:10:11,480
literally put my hand in here underneath the slurry and catch it, and you'll see as the

135
00:10:11,481 --> 00:10:16,680
water runs off, I have salt left in my hand.

136
00:10:16,681 --> 00:10:22,040
After the evaporation process, the salt still contains some water.

137
00:10:22,041 --> 00:10:25,020
So a filter wheel dries it off.

138
00:10:25,021 --> 00:10:28,320
The end result is common table salt.

139
00:10:28,321 --> 00:10:35,440
This process can also create the purest form of salt, 99.9% sodium chloride.

140
00:10:35,441 --> 00:10:43,080
It's used as saline salt in emergency rooms and is part of kidney dialysis.

141
00:10:43,081 --> 00:10:49,760
Yet another method of producing salt taps the rich resources not beneath the ground,

142
00:10:49,761 --> 00:10:51,265
the Earth's oceans.

143
00:10:51,266 --> 00:10:57,840
In Newark, California, south of San Francisco, Cargill operates a solar evaporation

144
00:10:57,841 --> 00:10:58,541
plant.

145
00:10:58,542 --> 00:11:06,720
Three pumps send as much as 30,000 gallons per minute of seawater from San Francisco Bay to

146
00:11:06,721 --> 00:11:11,572
solar evaporation ponds where the brine concentrates for four to five years.

147
00:11:11,573 --> 00:11:13,360
When ready, the concentrated

148
00:11:13,361 --> 00:11:19,120
brine travels to the facility's crystallizers where it dries forming crystals.

149
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:27,520
This is a fully saturated solution of brine water meaning that this water would not hold any more

150
00:11:27,521 --> 00:11:32,323
salt. It's approximately 25% sodium chloride, 75% water.

151
00:11:32,324 --> 00:11:35,840
The color of the brine is pink because of

152
00:11:35,841 --> 00:11:41,694
a microorganism that is in the brine that will only live at the salinity level.

153
00:11:41,695 --> 00:11:42,880
The crystallizer

154
00:11:42,881 --> 00:11:46,614
behind me is the final step in our process of making the salt.

155
00:11:46,615 --> 00:11:48,120
The salt water, once it's

156
00:11:48,121 --> 00:11:53,020
become saturated with sodium chloride, is pumped into the crystallizer, usually in the

157
00:11:53,021 --> 00:11:55,641
beginning of the summer months, what we call our making season.

158
00:11:55,642 --> 00:11:56,640
That's when the sunshine

159
00:11:56,641 --> 00:12:00,245
is the hottest and there's usually the most amount of wind in the summertime.

160
00:12:00,246 --> 00:12:00,946
The wind

161
00:12:00,947 --> 00:12:06,220
stirs up the brines. As it blows, there's actually wave action. What we've found is

162
00:12:06,221 --> 00:12:09,000
the more wind that blows, the bigger the crystals that will form.

163
00:12:09,001 --> 00:12:15,180
To gather the salt crystals, Cargill relies on a 20-ton piece of equipment called a harvester.

164
00:12:15,181 --> 00:12:18,280
There's actually a knife edge on the very bottom.

165
00:12:18,281 --> 00:12:24,520
It's seven feet wide, and it cuts about five inches of the salt.

166
00:12:24,521 --> 00:12:30,660
This is our largest crystallizer, where we harvest over 200,000 tons of salt.

167
00:12:30,661 --> 00:12:35,280
And we're going to be in this pond for about the next month harvesting salt.

168
00:12:35,281 --> 00:12:39,180
The salt is 99% purity even before it's been washed.

169
00:12:39,181 --> 00:12:40,820
It's actually safe to eat at this point.

170
00:12:43,480 --> 00:12:46,100
Tastes salty.

171
00:12:46,101 --> 00:12:49,800
Trucks take 30 tons of salt each to the wash plant,

172
00:12:49,801 --> 00:12:51,460
where it's dumped into a pit of brine water

173
00:12:51,461 --> 00:12:53,140
to remove any remaining impurities.

174
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:58,340
The salt then goes through a screw washer

175
00:12:58,341 --> 00:13:00,800
to dewater the crystals.

176
00:13:00,801 --> 00:13:03,940
The dewatering screen drains the wash brine,

177
00:13:03,941 --> 00:13:07,540
and the salt is clean and ready to stack.

178
00:13:07,541 --> 00:13:10,700
The 600,000 tons produced here annually

179
00:13:10,701 --> 00:13:14,940
will be going into more than just salt shakers.

180
00:13:14,941 --> 00:13:18,180
Surprisingly, of all the salt produced in the US,

181
00:13:18,181 --> 00:13:21,600
only 5% is used for food.

182
00:13:21,601 --> 00:13:25,420
The number one use, consuming nearly half of America's supply,

183
00:13:25,421 --> 00:13:27,700
just might save your life in the dead of winter.

184
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:33,740
Solution mining helped create vast underground salt caverns

185
00:13:33,741 --> 00:13:36,500
restoring the United States Strategic Petroleum Reserve.

186
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:41,560
Over 700 million barrels of oil can be held there.

187
00:13:42,440 --> 00:13:45,340
Salt will return on Modern Marvels.

188
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:49,380
Every year in the United States,

189
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:53,160
snow and ice cause 600,000 traffic accidents

190
00:13:53,161 --> 00:13:55,460
and 2,200 fatalities.

191
00:13:57,120 --> 00:13:58,580
To counter the problem,

192
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:02,800
workers apply about 16 million tons of salt on winter roads.

193
00:14:02,801 --> 00:14:06,660
taking advantage of salt's ability to melt through ice and snow.

194
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:11,240
More than half of all the salt produced in America,

195
00:14:12,040 --> 00:14:15,640
nine times more than we sprinkle on our food, is used for this purpose.

196
00:14:16,280 --> 00:14:20,880
That includes most of the salt you see here at Cargill's salt production facility in Cleveland.

197
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:28,560
I'd say about 95% of the salt we produce is exclusively for the icing market.

198
00:14:28,561 --> 00:14:34,240
The salt travels straight from the mine to professional de-icers, like those here at

199
00:14:34,241 --> 00:14:39,920
North Country Snow and Ice Management in Glens Falls, New York.

200
00:14:39,921 --> 00:14:46,880
It's only the first snow of the season, but they're already hard at work.

201
00:14:46,881 --> 00:14:52,800
The salt doesn't attach to the ice, but instead dissolves within it.

202
00:14:52,801 --> 00:14:57,040
Salt molecules replace some water molecules in the ice.

203
00:14:57,041 --> 00:15:00,180
decreasing the number of water molecules able to bond into ice crystals.

204
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:02,820
Once the ice becomes a salty solution,

205
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:06,700
the newly created brine will penetrate the remaining ice down to the pavement,

206
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:10,780
creating a brine sandwich with the road on bottom and the ice on top.

207
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:15,600
The brine lowers the freezing point of water from 32 degrees Fahrenheit

208
00:15:15,601 --> 00:15:18,280
to about 2 degrees, thawing some of the ice.

209
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:23,280
A plow then comes across the road, breaking up the ice so it can be removed.

210
00:15:23,281 --> 00:15:28,320
The most dangerous part of this job is not doing it right the first time.

211
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,620
Any miscalculation can be the difference between life and death on the road.

212
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:38,060
If I don't do my job, someone gets hurt, I probably don't have a job anymore.

213
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:42,080
And we try to make sure everyone is safe when we're dealing with ice.

214
00:15:42,860 --> 00:15:47,380
But using more and more salt doesn't necessarily equal a successful de-icing mission.

215
00:15:47,381 --> 00:15:54,380
The proper application rate is about 300 pounds, 250 pounds per acre.

216
00:15:54,381 --> 00:15:59,380
If you undersalt, the temperature at which salt works is going to increase.

217
00:15:59,381 --> 00:16:01,380
If you oversalt, the same thing.

218
00:16:01,381 --> 00:16:05,380
You are actually going to lose the effectiveness of the salt by putting too much down.

219
00:16:05,381 --> 00:16:09,380
So there is a sweet spot as far as application is concerned.

220
00:16:09,381 --> 00:16:14,380
Salt may be a cheap and efficient de-icing agent, but there's a downside.

221
00:16:14,381 --> 00:16:22,700
The salt water it leaves behind, especially on roads where salt is applied to liberally, can cause massive corrosion problems for cars.

222
00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:31,200
Automotive corrosion costs Americans in excess of $23 billion each year, or $115 for every motorist.

223
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:34,040
And the problem doesn't stop there.

224
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:40,640
The salt water can also seep into the seams of bridges and other metallic road structures, accelerating their corrosion.

225
00:16:40,641 --> 00:16:45,400
Cleveland's Cargill facility is doing its part to solve the problem

226
00:16:45,401 --> 00:16:49,460
by adding an extra chemical to the de-icing salt it produces.

227
00:16:50,220 --> 00:16:51,800
It's a product we make called clear lane.

228
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:55,020
There is a corrosion inhibitor in it, so on a bridge deck,

229
00:16:55,380 --> 00:17:00,140
if it gets into the cracks, it would not deteriorate the steel or the rebar in the bridge deck as quickly.

230
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:03,520
As prized as salt is for this purpose today,

231
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:10,120
2,000 years ago it was treasured as the most vital part of the human diet.

232
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:14,700
Since salt is essential to life, it became a prime mover,

233
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:17,360
inextricably tied to the march of history.

234
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:22,000
The military security of the Roman Empire hinged on it.

235
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:25,580
The Roman soldiers were paid in part in salt,

236
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:27,980
and from which we get the name salary.

237
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:32,800
Societies that controlled salt trade routes wielded vast economic power.

238
00:17:33,860 --> 00:17:37,220
In the 13th century, merchants in Venice gained control

239
00:17:37,221 --> 00:17:40,620
of an important maritime trade route passing through the Adriatic Sea.

240
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:47,340
The revenue they amassed from regulating salt traffic funded Venice's hydraulic system that prevented the city from washing away.

241
00:17:48,260 --> 00:17:52,920
Many of the major roads in Europe and Asia trace the same paths as the old salt trade routes.

242
00:17:55,540 --> 00:18:04,880
And in the Sahara Desert, merchants still transport salt mined from beneath the sands along the same route traveled by their ancestors a thousand years ago.

243
00:18:05,660 --> 00:18:08,060
But the salt then came at a steeper price.

244
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,340
It was traded at one point ounce for ounce with gold

245
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:14,200
because it was so hard to get.

246
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:17,260
At no time was salt more valued

247
00:18:17,261 --> 00:18:19,680
as a staple of human survival than in war.

248
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,160
During America's Civil War,

249
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:24,340
Union troops targeted salt storage

250
00:18:24,341 --> 00:18:26,260
and salt production facilities in the South,

251
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:29,500
knowing that if they cut off the salt supply,

252
00:18:29,740 --> 00:18:30,960
the Confederacy would wither.

253
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:33,420
But the North failed to disrupt enough

254
00:18:33,421 --> 00:18:35,380
of the Southern salt traffic to make a difference

255
00:18:35,381 --> 00:18:39,240
and had to win victory with more conventional strategies.

256
00:18:41,220 --> 00:18:45,480
Companies like Morton Salt know we need salt just as much now as we did then.

257
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:50,240
Some of these silos at its manufacturing facility in Silver Springs, New York,

258
00:18:51,080 --> 00:18:52,980
hold as much as 600 tons.

259
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:57,540
The salt produced here will not only find its way to homes and restaurants,

260
00:18:57,880 --> 00:19:01,240
but also into processed foods as a preservative and flavoring.

261
00:19:01,241 --> 00:19:09,560
A whopping 77% of the sodium Americans consume comes from eating processed foods, about 15

262
00:19:09,561 --> 00:19:14,840
times more than the amount we ingest by sprinkling it on our meals.

263
00:19:14,841 --> 00:19:20,360
This helps explain why each American consumes about 15 pounds of sodium each year, 50% more

264
00:19:20,361 --> 00:19:24,200
than the USDA's maximum recommendation.

265
00:19:24,201 --> 00:19:27,480
This excess of salt can contribute to strokes and hypertension.

266
00:19:27,481 --> 00:19:32,480
But on the plus side for salt, since 1924, Morton and some other salt makers have been

267
00:19:32,481 --> 00:19:37,100
adding a key element to help keep you healthy, iodine.

268
00:19:37,101 --> 00:19:42,260
We all need iodine, mostly because it helps regulate the thyroid gland, which controls

269
00:19:42,261 --> 00:19:44,140
how quickly the body burns energy.

270
00:19:44,141 --> 00:19:49,720
Iodizing salt was the great public health achievement along with sanitizing water in

271
00:19:49,721 --> 00:19:50,980
the 20th century.

272
00:19:50,981 --> 00:19:55,540
Although we'd known for a century before that that iodine was important to the function

273
00:19:55,541 --> 00:19:59,840
the thyroid gland, we didn't understand that salt could so

274
00:19:59,841 --> 00:20:01,880
readily satisfy that need.

275
00:20:01,881 --> 00:20:04,020
People eat a predictable amount of salt,

276
00:20:04,021 --> 00:20:07,520
so you can meter the amount of iodine that they get by putting

277
00:20:07,521 --> 00:20:10,580
it in the salt, knowing how much salt they'll have.

278
00:20:10,581 --> 00:20:12,520
We've all added a little salt to a meal

279
00:20:12,521 --> 00:20:15,200
for just the right flavor.

280
00:20:15,201 --> 00:20:18,200
But prior to refrigeration, adding salt was the best way

281
00:20:18,201 --> 00:20:21,000
to keep food from spoiling.

282
00:20:21,001 --> 00:20:24,060
Salt preserves food by absorbing the moisture bacteria need

283
00:20:24,061 --> 00:20:27,220
to multiply.

284
00:20:27,221 --> 00:20:29,340
Some historians contend that the United States

285
00:20:29,341 --> 00:20:31,940
owes its very existence to salt's value as a food

286
00:20:31,941 --> 00:20:32,641
preservative.

287
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:38,120
Before the Revolution, the American colonies prospered

288
00:20:38,121 --> 00:20:41,220
and grew in part because salt made possible the lucrative North

289
00:20:41,221 --> 00:20:44,360
Atlantic cod industry.

290
00:20:44,361 --> 00:20:46,860
Salt preserves cod unusually well,

291
00:20:46,861 --> 00:20:50,020
because its flesh is almost entirely devoid of fat.

292
00:20:50,021 --> 00:20:52,740
Fat resists salt, slowing the rate of preservation.

293
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:58,280
Using salt, colonial fishermen were able to preserve and export vast amounts of cod,

294
00:20:59,620 --> 00:21:03,960
helping to power the economy of a young America, surging toward independence.

295
00:21:04,980 --> 00:21:07,760
And the proven preserver still comes in handy today.

296
00:21:08,600 --> 00:21:11,100
Salt is used in foods today as a preservative.

297
00:21:11,440 --> 00:21:18,220
Hams and foods like that that are cured in salt are done for the same reason of not only imparting flavor,

298
00:21:18,221 --> 00:21:20,720
but to have a preserving effect.

299
00:21:20,820 --> 00:21:23,720
Still, our closest encounter with salt on a daily basis

300
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:27,660
is when we reach for the shaker.

301
00:21:27,760 --> 00:21:29,760
And there's one restaurant where salting your food

302
00:21:29,860 --> 00:21:32,000
is no insult to the chef.

303
00:21:32,100 --> 00:21:34,640
He encourages it.

304
00:21:34,740 --> 00:21:38,540
Located in the Ritz-Carlton Hotel on Amelia Island, Florida,

305
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:43,620
salt incorporates salt into every aspect of the meal,

306
00:21:43,720 --> 00:21:47,780
from food prep to cooking to flavoring at the table.

307
00:21:47,781 --> 00:21:51,500
But when you say pass the salt here, you'd better be more specific.

308
00:21:52,720 --> 00:21:56,340
At any given time, we have about 40 different salts in the restaurant.

309
00:21:57,140 --> 00:21:58,900
They come from all around the world.

310
00:21:59,640 --> 00:22:01,720
Some of the salts are made different by nature,

311
00:22:02,380 --> 00:22:05,000
which has added minerals to the deposits from which they're mined.

312
00:22:06,140 --> 00:22:08,080
Others owe their character to processes

313
00:22:08,081 --> 00:22:11,100
in which common salt is mixed with ingredients like clay

314
00:22:11,101 --> 00:22:14,920
or undergoes treatments like wood smoking.

315
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:18,220
Activated charcoal is turned this salt black.

316
00:22:18,860 --> 00:22:21,960
And the large pyramid-shaped crystals make for a fun crunch.

317
00:22:22,900 --> 00:22:24,780
This salt is cypress flake salt.

318
00:22:25,340 --> 00:22:27,500
This is harvested from the Black Sea in the Mediterranean.

319
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:31,280
We use this a lot of times either in salads or raw preparations

320
00:22:31,281 --> 00:22:34,400
because the quick pop of salt, but also the texture.

321
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:38,880
Some salts here, like this one that contains a bamboo flavor,

322
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:40,960
go beyond spicing up your meal.

323
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:44,460
This is the Japanese aguni salt.

324
00:22:44,461 --> 00:22:46,820
comes from a barrier island off the coast of Japan,

325
00:22:46,821 --> 00:22:48,060
Aguni Island.

326
00:22:48,061 --> 00:22:50,560
What we use this for in the restaurant

327
00:22:50,561 --> 00:22:52,500
is mainly with our coffee.

328
00:22:52,501 --> 00:22:56,080
When it's added to coffee, it actually lowers the acidity

329
00:22:56,081 --> 00:22:59,080
and mellows out the bitterness of coffee.

330
00:22:59,081 --> 00:23:02,440
One of the salts here, created by the restaurant chefs,

331
00:23:02,441 --> 00:23:05,220
even adds a little flavor to the desserts.

332
00:23:05,221 --> 00:23:07,680
What we have here is just a little bit of dried lavender

333
00:23:07,681 --> 00:23:10,380
that we're going to put into our coffee grinder,

334
00:23:10,381 --> 00:23:13,460
a little bit of Bolivian rose because it's very delicate salt,

335
00:23:13,461 --> 00:23:16,540
and lavender is a very delicate, fragrant flower.

336
00:23:17,260 --> 00:23:18,520
Basically, you just have a coffee grinder,

337
00:23:19,420 --> 00:23:20,880
your salt and your lavender in there.

338
00:23:21,460 --> 00:23:23,380
Just keeping blending it until the lavender

339
00:23:23,381 --> 00:23:25,080
has been fully incorporated into the salt.

340
00:23:27,160 --> 00:23:29,760
What we're left with is a fragrant Bolivian rose

341
00:23:29,761 --> 00:23:34,520
that we could use on sorbet, an ice cream, a dessert.

342
00:23:35,340 --> 00:23:37,740
As you're smacking your lips at the sight of all the salt here,

343
00:23:38,480 --> 00:23:40,200
are you wondering what kind is the priciest?

344
00:23:40,201 --> 00:23:45,200
The most expensive one that I have come across is a Danish Viking salt.

345
00:23:45,201 --> 00:23:47,200
It's about $150 a pound.

346
00:23:47,201 --> 00:23:51,200
We don't necessarily use it in the restaurant because of the cost of it.

347
00:23:51,201 --> 00:23:54,200
The restaurant's salt philosophy runs so deep,

348
00:23:54,201 --> 00:23:57,200
it's literally in the very air you breathe.

349
00:23:57,201 --> 00:24:02,200
Out in the front of our room, we feature a Himalayan salt piece.

350
00:24:02,201 --> 00:24:06,200
As the salt heats up, it actually ionizes the air,

351
00:24:06,201 --> 00:24:09,200
and it's supposed to make you feel happier, a little bit stress-free.

352
00:24:09,201 --> 00:24:14,660
stress-free, but also it's kind of the centerpiece of what we're trying to represent, you know,

353
00:24:14,700 --> 00:24:16,398
a journey through salt.

354
00:24:16,399 --> 00:24:21,860
As much as we crave and value salt, it also makes our most abundant source

355
00:24:21,861 --> 00:24:23,059
of water undrinkable.

356
00:24:23,060 --> 00:24:27,340
But if you're willing to pay a $10 million electric bill, technology offers

357
00:24:27,341 --> 00:24:28,467
a solution.

358
00:24:28,468 --> 00:24:37,380
In ancient Greece, merchants would trade slaves for salt, giving rise to the expression

359
00:24:37,381 --> 00:24:39,980
that a man was not worth his salt.

360
00:24:41,380 --> 00:24:44,080
Salt will return on Modern Marvels.

361
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:49,220
Oceans cover over 70% of the Earth's surface,

362
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:53,520
a staggering 326 million cubic miles of water.

363
00:24:54,520 --> 00:24:56,580
And all of it is filled with salt.

364
00:24:57,360 --> 00:24:59,140
If you gathered up all that salt,

365
00:24:59,680 --> 00:25:02,420
it would cover the continents to a depth of 500 feet.

366
00:25:03,740 --> 00:25:05,340
Conceptually, that's a fitting image,

367
00:25:05,341 --> 00:25:09,020
because where do you think the salt in the oceans came from in the first place?

368
00:25:10,440 --> 00:25:13,320
Ultimately, the salt that is in the ocean came from the land.

369
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:17,720
Salt journeyed to the oceans thanks to rain.

370
00:25:19,140 --> 00:25:23,160
When rain falls, the surrounding air causes it to be slightly acidic.

371
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:28,080
As it strikes land, the acid in the rain then erodes and breaks down rocks.

372
00:25:28,740 --> 00:25:34,540
The runoff carries dissolved salts to streams and rivers, which lead to the ocean.

373
00:25:35,340 --> 00:25:42,340
Only fresh water in the ocean evaporates, returning to the atmosphere.

374
00:25:42,341 --> 00:25:48,340
Since the salt is too heavy to evaporate, it stays put.

375
00:25:48,341 --> 00:25:53,340
The ocean's salt content is 3.5%.

376
00:25:53,341 --> 00:25:56,340
But that's nothing compared to 20,000 years ago,

377
00:25:56,341 --> 00:25:59,340
when scientists estimate it was nearly double that.

378
00:25:59,341 --> 00:26:01,340
That's because a sizable portion of the Earth's fresh water

379
00:26:01,341 --> 00:26:04,340
was locked up in glaciers during the last ice age.

380
00:26:04,341 --> 00:26:07,820
And that left less water to dilute the salt entering the ocean.

381
00:26:09,380 --> 00:26:12,760
Today's ocean salt content is more than twice that of the fish that live there,

382
00:26:13,240 --> 00:26:16,980
which have a salinity of 1.5%.

383
00:26:16,981 --> 00:26:20,560
But the fish can drink the water and survive

384
00:26:20,561 --> 00:26:24,780
because their gills are able to process the water, removing some of the salt.

385
00:26:25,780 --> 00:26:27,940
As the evolutionary descendants of fish,

386
00:26:28,600 --> 00:26:31,800
we humans have a slightly lower salinity, 0.9%.

387
00:26:31,801 --> 00:26:37,640
So although we need and can easily ingest salt, drinking salt water is very harmful to us.

388
00:26:38,140 --> 00:26:44,940
You can't drink salt water because drinking salt water would actually dehydrate you, cause kidney failure, and you would die.

389
00:26:45,660 --> 00:26:50,180
You would have to drink about one gallon of salt water for your body to cross the fatal threshold.

390
00:26:51,420 --> 00:26:59,120
The only way to make ocean water safe for human consumption, short of developing gills, is a process known as desalination.

391
00:26:59,121 --> 00:27:06,560
Tampa, Florida is home to the largest seawater desalination plant in North America.

392
00:27:06,561 --> 00:27:09,880
It provides water for 2.5 million people.

393
00:27:09,881 --> 00:27:13,560
The plant here is co-located with a power plant.

394
00:27:13,561 --> 00:27:18,660
In their process of making electricity, they take 1.4 billion gallons from Tampa Bay and

395
00:27:18,661 --> 00:27:20,480
use it to cool their boilers.

396
00:27:20,481 --> 00:27:25,540
We take 44 million gallons and make 25 million gallons of drinking water.

397
00:27:25,541 --> 00:27:30,160
Before the facility takes on the task of removing the salt from the seawater, the water must

398
00:27:30,161 --> 00:27:35,140
travel through a series of filters to remove other impurities, leaving only the salt in

399
00:27:35,141 --> 00:27:37,180
the water.

400
00:27:37,181 --> 00:27:40,100
Now comes the most crucial step of all.

401
00:27:40,101 --> 00:27:45,660
That water is now ready to go into the reverse osmosis building, which is right behind me.

402
00:27:45,661 --> 00:27:49,060
It is forced through these long tubes.

403
00:27:49,061 --> 00:27:52,760
Each one of those tubes has membranes in it.

404
00:27:52,761 --> 00:27:55,320
This is a cutaway of the membranes.

405
00:27:55,840 --> 00:27:59,820
This is actually about half of the length of a full membrane.

406
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:03,040
The water is put under high pressure, about 1,000 psi,

407
00:28:03,380 --> 00:28:06,400
and it's pushed through this end of the membrane.

408
00:28:07,220 --> 00:28:10,760
The intense pressure forces the water across the spiral membrane,

409
00:28:11,100 --> 00:28:14,640
which consists of multiple layers dotted with molecular-sized pores.

410
00:28:16,040 --> 00:28:19,020
The salt particles are too large to pass through the pores,

411
00:28:19,021 --> 00:28:25,380
but the pure water molecules are small enough to fit through and flow into a collector tube at the

412
00:28:25,381 --> 00:28:27,154
end of the membrane.

413
00:28:27,155 --> 00:28:33,720
The salty concentrate exits through openings on the side and pumps move 19

414
00:28:33,721 --> 00:28:39,420
million gallons of the concentrate per day back to the electric power plant to be diluted with

415
00:28:39,421 --> 00:28:42,369
salt water before it's returned to the bay.

416
00:28:42,370 --> 00:28:46,140
After the water has undergone a final round of chemical

417
00:28:46,141 --> 00:28:49,620
treatment, the plant has met its daily goal of producing 25

418
00:28:49,621 --> 00:28:52,680
million gallons of drinkable water.

419
00:28:52,681 --> 00:28:55,260
Here we are at the end of the desalination facility.

420
00:28:55,261 --> 00:28:57,600
Hard to believe that only one hour ago,

421
00:28:57,601 --> 00:28:59,960
that water entered the plant from the bay.

422
00:28:59,961 --> 00:29:06,120
And now I can tap it and drink it.

423
00:29:06,121 --> 00:29:08,640
Cheers.

424
00:29:08,641 --> 00:29:11,200
But desalinated water has a downside.

425
00:29:11,201 --> 00:29:14,420
One of the things about doing seawater desal is the cost.

426
00:29:14,421 --> 00:29:16,760
It's the operating costs here that are very expensive.

427
00:29:17,320 --> 00:29:20,000
We have a $17 million budget to run this plant.

428
00:29:20,380 --> 00:29:23,200
Almost $10 million of that is just to pay our electric bill.

429
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:28,140
To ease energy costs, the plant utilizes a reusable energy technique.

430
00:29:30,280 --> 00:29:33,800
After the salty concentrate exits the reverse osmosis membrane,

431
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:37,540
it maintains a pressure of over 900 PSI.

432
00:29:38,520 --> 00:29:42,620
The high pressure is used to spin a turbine, creating electricity.

433
00:29:42,621 --> 00:29:45,620
We are getting an upwards of 35% energy recovery

434
00:29:45,720 --> 00:29:47,360
from that system.

435
00:29:47,460 --> 00:29:49,160
Still, desalinated water remains

436
00:29:49,260 --> 00:29:51,960
the most expensive to produce.

437
00:29:52,060 --> 00:29:56,460
It costs the company $3.38 per 1,000 gallons,

438
00:29:56,560 --> 00:29:59,800
compared to its groundwater production at about $1

439
00:29:59,900 --> 00:30:04,280
and its surface water at about $2.

440
00:30:04,380 --> 00:30:08,280
Although it may only account for 10% of Tampa's drinking water,

441
00:30:08,380 --> 00:30:10,820
desalinated water currently stands apart

442
00:30:10,821 --> 00:30:13,820
as one of the very few drought-proof sources.

443
00:30:13,821 --> 00:30:18,820
I do think back to the famous John F. Kennedy quotation

444
00:30:18,821 --> 00:30:23,820
about if we could find a way to economically desalinate water,

445
00:30:23,821 --> 00:30:27,820
that it would be one of the greatest achievements of mankind.

446
00:30:27,821 --> 00:30:32,820
And we've done it on a scale that we haven't seen in the United States.

447
00:30:32,821 --> 00:30:38,820
As of 2008, the Tampa plant is the only large-scale desalination facility in the U.S.,

448
00:30:38,821 --> 00:30:42,360
in the U.S., but other coastal states are exploring the possibilities.

449
00:30:44,360 --> 00:30:47,080
California alone has plans for as many as 20 plants.

450
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:53,560
There are times when adding salt to water can be just as beneficial as removing it.

451
00:30:54,620 --> 00:30:56,500
Case in point, swimming pools.

452
00:30:57,100 --> 00:31:00,240
The conventional method of disinfecting the water is to add chlorine.

453
00:31:01,100 --> 00:31:05,760
But too much chlorine in a pool will irritate your eyes and can even make you sick.

454
00:31:05,761 --> 00:31:14,720
In Moorpark, California, Jandy Pool Products makes a revolutionary device that makes it virtually impossible to over-chlorinate your pool.

455
00:31:15,320 --> 00:31:16,640
A salt chlorinator.

456
00:31:17,680 --> 00:31:19,052
The key to its operation?

457
00:31:19,053 --> 00:31:22,400
Breaking down common table salt to generate its own chlorine.

458
00:31:23,360 --> 00:31:28,740
Simply add 300 pounds of ordinary table salt to the pool and let it dissolve.

459
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:33,600
It flows into the chlorinator, positioned between the pool's filter and outlet.

460
00:31:33,601 --> 00:31:38,820
electrodes powered by low DC voltage emit an electric current that splits the

461
00:31:38,821 --> 00:31:44,100
salt into sodium and chlorine over time the small amounts of chlorine return to

462
00:31:44,101 --> 00:31:52,280
the pool and kill any bacteria algae or viruses we do add about 300 pounds of

463
00:31:52,281 --> 00:31:55,720
salt to the average pool but it's not enough salt to reach your taste

464
00:31:55,721 --> 00:32:01,380
threshold so it tastes like ordinary water to give you a comparison what's

465
00:32:01,381 --> 00:32:03,859
only one-third the saltiness of your tears.

466
00:32:03,860 --> 00:32:06,280
And because the salinity is closer to your

467
00:32:06,281 --> 00:32:08,525
own tears, there's no eye irritation.

468
00:32:08,526 --> 00:32:11,620
And unlike old-fashioned pools, there's no chlorine

469
00:32:11,621 --> 00:32:13,560
smell.

470
00:32:13,561 --> 00:32:16,639
But how does the system prevent you from over-chlorinating?

471
00:32:16,640 --> 00:32:19,040
Once the chlorine has done its job, it doesn't

472
00:32:19,041 --> 00:32:21,360
hang around in the pool.

473
00:32:21,361 --> 00:32:24,830
A saltwater pool is a model of recycling efficiency.

474
00:32:24,831 --> 00:32:27,700
It reuses the salt molecules over and over.

475
00:32:27,701 --> 00:32:32,500
The chlorine does the sanitizing, and when it's done doing the sanitizing, recombines

476
00:32:32,501 --> 00:32:34,820
with the sodium to become salt again.

477
00:32:34,821 --> 00:32:40,180
But salt can do a lot more for you than make pool fun go more swimmingly.

478
00:32:40,181 --> 00:32:45,460
Would you believe this chunk of salt can be the key to electrifying your house?

479
00:32:45,461 --> 00:32:50,380
The saltiest body of water in the world is Lake Assal in the Republic of Djibouti in

480
00:32:50,381 --> 00:32:51,620
Africa.

481
00:32:51,621 --> 00:32:57,580
It contains a maximum 25% salt in solution and an additional 10% that can't dissolve.

482
00:32:57,581 --> 00:33:02,380
10 times saltier than the ocean. Salt will return on Modern Marbles.

483
00:33:04,700 --> 00:33:08,240
Just like metals, salts can melt if you get them hot enough.

484
00:33:10,100 --> 00:33:14,680
That makes them perfect for an industrial application known as the melanite process.

485
00:33:14,681 --> 00:33:18,043
It's been around since the early 20th century.

486
00:33:18,044 --> 00:33:21,260
Burlington Engineering in Orange, California

487
00:33:21,261 --> 00:33:25,722
uses the melanite process to add a layer of protection to steel parts.

488
00:33:25,723 --> 00:33:27,060
It increases hardness

489
00:33:27,061 --> 00:33:29,020
and slows corrosion and wear.

490
00:33:30,600 --> 00:33:32,740
Melaniting takes two varieties of salt,

491
00:33:33,100 --> 00:33:34,500
but neither is sodium chloride.

492
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:36,700
However, they are cousins of salt.

493
00:33:38,280 --> 00:33:40,880
They both contain sodium as the metal component,

494
00:33:41,420 --> 00:33:43,520
but it's bound to nitrogen in one salt

495
00:33:43,521 --> 00:33:45,000
and carbon in the other.

496
00:33:46,240 --> 00:33:48,880
During the process, nitrogen and carbon

497
00:33:48,881 --> 00:33:50,880
penetrate the surface of the steel parts,

498
00:33:51,720 --> 00:33:52,600
fusing with the steel.

499
00:33:52,601 --> 00:34:00,240
We see the melanite process used in a huge variety of industries, anywhere from automotive

500
00:34:00,241 --> 00:34:06,340
and using it on things like crankshafts to give longer wear life on bearing surfaces

501
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:07,303
and camshafts.

502
00:34:07,304 --> 00:34:12,460
We do aftermarket automotive work where we do all different types and sizes

503
00:34:12,461 --> 00:34:16,699
of flywheels that will wear longer and last longer.

504
00:34:16,700 --> 00:34:19,940
We see it in the oil drilling industry.

505
00:34:19,941 --> 00:34:24,700
We have parts that go out on oil rigs out in the middle of the ocean and out in the middle of the desert.

506
00:34:25,240 --> 00:34:33,820
And so they have to hold up in a very severe environment where not only wear resistance, but corrosion resistance is very important.

507
00:34:34,880 --> 00:34:41,860
The first step in the process is we take the parts and we put them into racks or fixtures, and then we take them and we wash them.

508
00:34:43,340 --> 00:34:47,600
The parts travel to a preheat oven where they'll be heated to 800 degrees Fahrenheit.

509
00:34:47,601 --> 00:34:56,120
without this step the parts could deform in the intense heat of the salt bath so now the parts

510
00:34:56,121 --> 00:35:00,620
have finished going through the preheat process and now that they're at 800 degrees we're gonna

511
00:35:00,621 --> 00:35:07,840
bring them around and immerse them into the salt bath nitriding bath the parts will remain in this

512
00:35:07,841 --> 00:35:15,080
initial bath for 90 minutes so after we've gone through the first step of the SPN bath we now

513
00:35:15,081 --> 00:35:18,920
into the second step, we're diffusing nitrogen and carbon

514
00:35:19,020 --> 00:35:20,620
primarily into the steel.

515
00:35:20,720 --> 00:35:22,620
And this is where the carbon goes in.

516
00:35:22,720 --> 00:35:26,720
And it'll be in this bath for approximately 30 minutes.

517
00:35:26,820 --> 00:35:29,820
A water quench completes the process.

518
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:32,740
But some steel parts need an even smoother surface

519
00:35:32,840 --> 00:35:36,740
or more corrosion resistance and require a polish.

520
00:35:36,840 --> 00:35:39,540
For that, they get all shook up.

521
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:41,980
We'll take parts and we'll put them in a vibratory tub,

522
00:35:42,080 --> 00:35:44,840
which polishes the parts and moves them around.

523
00:35:44,841 --> 00:35:47,240
depending on the parts that we're polishing,

524
00:35:47,340 --> 00:35:49,840
sometimes we'll use a media like this,

525
00:35:49,940 --> 00:35:54,380
which is a corncob media that's impregnating with resin.

526
00:35:54,480 --> 00:35:56,680
The melanite process improves some items

527
00:35:56,780 --> 00:35:58,780
in surprising ways.

528
00:35:58,880 --> 00:36:01,020
The molten salt baths can smooth out the interior

529
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:04,960
of a gun barrel, resulting in less friction for the bullets.

530
00:36:05,060 --> 00:36:08,560
If it has a lower drag coefficient, it gets less hot.

531
00:36:08,660 --> 00:36:11,400
And a gun barrel that is less hot is more accurate.

532
00:36:14,840 --> 00:36:21,840
Of their products, the folks at Burlington Engineering like to say the same thing automaker Henry Ford once said about his Model T.

533
00:36:23,160 --> 00:36:25,500
It's any color you want as long as it's black.

534
00:36:26,440 --> 00:36:32,220
One of molten salt's properties, its ability to retain intense heat over long periods of time,

535
00:36:33,800 --> 00:36:36,920
might just revolutionize the world of renewable energy.

536
00:36:36,921 --> 00:36:45,480
An innovative system using molten salt as an energy storage device called Solar Trace is now under construction in Spain.

537
00:36:47,220 --> 00:36:56,840
It's based on the success of an earlier prototype, Solar II, which operated in California's Mojave Desert between 1995 and 1999.

538
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:05,060
While sodium chloride becomes molten at 1,474 degrees Fahrenheit and could easily do the job,

539
00:37:05,061 --> 00:37:10,060
Engineers incorporated other salts into Solar II's operations

540
00:37:10,061 --> 00:37:14,060
because they melt at a much lower temperature.

541
00:37:14,061 --> 00:37:21,060
A 300-foot hollow tower was encircled by more than 1,900 mirrors called heliostats.

542
00:37:21,061 --> 00:37:27,060
The heliostats tracked the sun, reflecting its energy to a receiver mounted on the tower.

543
00:37:27,061 --> 00:37:34,060
The receiver transmitted intense heat inside the tower to salt composed of sodium nitrate and potassium nitrate.

544
00:37:34,061 --> 00:37:39,360
At over a thousand degrees Fahrenheit, the salt became molten and flowed to a storage tank.

545
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:43,740
To produce energy, pumps conveyed the salt through a steam generator,

546
00:37:44,180 --> 00:37:47,520
consisting of a water tank and piping similar to a radiator.

547
00:37:48,140 --> 00:37:51,280
The steam then spun turbines, generating electrical energy.

548
00:37:52,540 --> 00:37:55,160
The system could produce up to 10 megawatts,

549
00:37:56,380 --> 00:37:58,200
enough to power 10,000 homes,

550
00:37:59,960 --> 00:38:03,340
using mineral salts no different than common garden fertilizer.

551
00:38:04,060 --> 00:38:12,820
The salts allowed for energy to remain stored when clouds covered the sun or at night.

552
00:38:12,821 --> 00:38:18,460
Once operational, solar trace will produce 17 megawatts.

553
00:38:18,461 --> 00:38:25,000
As the world continues to go green, salt promises to spice things up.

554
00:38:25,001 --> 00:38:27,500
Molten salts can hold energy.

555
00:38:27,501 --> 00:38:36,760
But if you're looking to use some energy of your own at record-breaking speeds, nothing beats an open salt flat.

556
00:38:41,620 --> 00:38:47,460
Molten salt is used as an electrolyte in thermal batteries that power guidance systems in short-range missiles.

557
00:38:48,080 --> 00:38:50,380
The salt melts and the battery becomes active.

558
00:38:51,040 --> 00:38:53,080
Shortly before or after, the missile is launched.

559
00:38:53,600 --> 00:38:55,860
Battery life is usually less than 10 minutes.

560
00:38:56,700 --> 00:38:58,720
Salt will return on Modern Marvels.

561
00:39:00,840 --> 00:39:04,780
You may think the ocean is salty at 3.5% salinity,

562
00:39:05,360 --> 00:39:09,360
but that's nothing compared to the Great Salt Lake in Utah.

563
00:39:10,700 --> 00:39:13,400
The southern half of the lake is 15%,

564
00:39:13,401 --> 00:39:18,280
and the north is 25%, seven times saltier than the ocean.

565
00:39:19,360 --> 00:39:23,600
The Great Salt Lake is the largest inland body of salt water in the Western Hemisphere.

566
00:39:23,601 --> 00:39:26,200
a railroad causeway bisects the lake,

567
00:39:26,860 --> 00:39:29,000
preventing fresh water from entering the north side.

568
00:39:30,220 --> 00:39:31,400
Through openings in the causeway,

569
00:39:31,740 --> 00:39:34,100
only concentrated salt water from the southern half

570
00:39:34,101 --> 00:39:36,140
can enter the northern half of the lake.

571
00:39:37,260 --> 00:39:39,920
The difference in salinity gives each half of the lake

572
00:39:39,921 --> 00:39:42,460
unique properties, including different colors

573
00:39:42,461 --> 00:39:44,160
and even different life forms.

574
00:39:45,160 --> 00:39:46,680
In the south half of the lake,

575
00:39:46,980 --> 00:39:48,840
you get a blue-green color to it.

576
00:39:49,540 --> 00:39:53,160
That's usually due to the blue-green algae.

577
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:59,340
When you get over the north half of the lake, it will be an absolutely pink or a red color,

578
00:39:59,700 --> 00:40:02,620
and that is due to the halophilic bacteria.

579
00:40:04,120 --> 00:40:07,280
The lake's high salinity makes life for fish impossible.

580
00:40:07,860 --> 00:40:12,480
But for more than a century, that hasn't prevented the tourist industry from taking advantage of the area.

581
00:40:13,140 --> 00:40:16,560
For years and years, this was a favorite place for resorts.

582
00:40:17,120 --> 00:40:21,620
The most famous of the lake's resorts, Salt Air, opened in 1893.

583
00:40:21,621 --> 00:40:26,360
Now a museum, it was once billed as a Coney Island of the West.

584
00:40:27,080 --> 00:40:30,100
It peaked in the 1920s and closed in the 50s.

585
00:40:31,240 --> 00:40:34,740
While the resorts had their amusements, the real fun was in the lake itself.

586
00:40:36,400 --> 00:40:41,420
Swimmers discovered that the rich salt content made them so buoyant, it was impossible to sink.

587
00:40:43,000 --> 00:40:45,940
The Great Salt Lake is all that remains of Lake Bonneville,

588
00:40:46,460 --> 00:40:50,080
a vast freshwater lake that existed some 30,000 years ago.

589
00:40:50,081 --> 00:40:57,840
It covered 20,000 square miles stretching from Nevada to Idaho and 100 miles from Salt Lake,

590
00:40:57,841 --> 00:41:02,900
where its waters once rippled, is now the sterile emptiness of the Bonneville Salt Flats.

591
00:41:02,901 --> 00:41:09,280
When it went away over the course of thousands of years, what was left behind were the salts that had

592
00:41:09,281 --> 00:41:10,906
been dissolved in the water.

593
00:41:10,907 --> 00:41:14,740
It's become kind of a focal point for a lot of folks as they think

594
00:41:14,741 --> 00:41:16,860
about the United States from throughout the world.

595
00:41:17,240 --> 00:41:18,660
They want to see the salt flats.

596
00:41:20,420 --> 00:41:21,880
Though a tourist attraction,

597
00:41:22,320 --> 00:41:25,820
the desolate 160 square miles of the flats pose a danger,

598
00:41:26,420 --> 00:41:28,260
without adequate water, food, and equipment.

599
00:41:29,660 --> 00:41:32,200
Anyone getting stuck out here would never survive.

600
00:41:32,940 --> 00:41:34,680
The land is so smooth and flat,

601
00:41:34,960 --> 00:41:36,460
you can see the curvature of the earth.

602
00:41:39,260 --> 00:41:40,540
But instead of danger,

603
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:43,640
some see the vast open flats and think one thing.

604
00:41:44,740 --> 00:41:45,740
Speed.

605
00:41:45,741 --> 00:41:53,540
You might think the salt creates great traction, but the opposite is true.

606
00:41:53,541 --> 00:41:58,460
Salt decreases traction by 12 to 15 percent, making driving on it a bit similar to driving

607
00:41:58,461 --> 00:42:01,140
on snow or ice.

608
00:42:01,141 --> 00:42:05,060
But unlike normal roads and highways, you won't find any potholes here.

609
00:42:05,061 --> 00:42:08,980
If any holes do occur, the solution is simple.

610
00:42:08,981 --> 00:42:12,300
Just pack in more salt.

611
00:42:12,301 --> 00:42:15,680
come from all over for the World of Speed Week, held every September.

612
00:42:16,340 --> 00:42:21,040
It's a time when everyday drivers and motorcyclists get to open up their engines to see just how

613
00:42:21,041 --> 00:42:21,900
fast they can go.

614
00:42:22,900 --> 00:42:27,560
Well, the first year we came out in 2001, we went like 196.

615
00:42:28,440 --> 00:42:33,200
And then it took four more years to get the last four miles an hour.

616
00:42:33,720 --> 00:42:38,280
And once you go 200 out here, then you want to go a little faster.

617
00:42:38,860 --> 00:42:40,660
We started out here about 2003.

618
00:42:40,661 --> 00:42:44,780
We've been about here every year since then, so two or three times a year.

619
00:42:45,400 --> 00:42:47,060
We've had the car up to 220.

620
00:42:47,900 --> 00:42:51,280
The Salt Flats have long been a destination for speed record setters.

621
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:57,060
John Cobb set the first of many land speed records at the Flats in 1935,

622
00:42:58,340 --> 00:43:01,180
a blistering 368 miles per hour.

623
00:43:05,400 --> 00:43:09,240
Since then, many drivers have made the pilgrimage to the Salt Flats

624
00:43:09,241 --> 00:43:11,300
with the intent of setting new records.

625
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:20,680
The drivers we caught up with weren't all expecting to set records

626
00:43:20,681 --> 00:43:24,140
or for their cars to win any beauty contests.

627
00:43:24,600 --> 00:43:25,920
Here's the way I feel about it.

628
00:43:26,140 --> 00:43:28,720
I only care about how fast it goes, not how pretty it looks.

629
00:43:29,180 --> 00:43:31,080
The course has a little ruts in it.

630
00:43:31,560 --> 00:43:33,380
The car likes to drift a little on the course,

631
00:43:33,660 --> 00:43:37,820
and it really makes you feel better if it will go where you point it.

632
00:43:39,240 --> 00:43:48,660
Still, they're quite happy to take advantage of yet another surprising dimension of salt.

633
00:43:48,661 --> 00:43:52,220
The salt is an incredibly abundant resource, but it's not endless.

634
00:43:52,221 --> 00:43:57,540
And we have to manage this in such a manner that future generations can come out here

635
00:43:57,541 --> 00:44:01,480
and marvel at the same thing that you're marveling at today.

636
00:44:01,481 --> 00:44:07,620
The salt abundance of these flats belies its scarcity throughout most of human history.

637
00:44:07,621 --> 00:44:11,420
So the next time you're asked, don't just pass the salt.

638
00:44:11,421 --> 00:44:14,660
Pause and be thankful your salary isn't just the contents

639
00:44:14,661 --> 00:44:16,860
of the shaker in your hand.

